y at the  pilgrimage to the Vatican to actually  celebrate 30 years of a major event  which started the Velvet Revolution in  Czechoslovakia and this is the patron  Agnes who was actually starting the  events in Czechoslovakia vieira marish  miss Lloyd Chuck excellencies guests  it's a great honor to be here and to  have the opportunity to tell you how  great we are how grateful we are for the  way we can celebrate 30 years after this  content and rebellion fight for freedom  and democracy 30 years ago we saw a  turning point between talk and action  and that was Czechoslovak contribution  to the world  we brought the name the term Velvet  Revolution to the existence no single  bullet was shot and that was unheard of  unimaginable when you would also imagine  that the evil of empire the the empire  which wasn't collapsing so easily was  still there and without any drop any any  blood being shed that is what was  happening The Velvet Revolution or  sometimes Slovaks and others call it  gentle revolution was later applied as a  tremor to describe the revolutions in  Poland Hungary East Germany  over the course of all those all those  years and months 30 years on what  happened in Europe in 1989 became a  model for other nonviolent pro-democracy  movements which have triumphed in many  countries since then tamati Gordon - the  liberal thinker and essayist and a  scholar in his very famous book year of  miracles who described it in a very  clear dynamics as it happened what took  polls ten years it took hungarians ten  months it took Eastern Germans ten weeks  and in a way it took czechoslovaks ten  days the symbol of demonstrations  besides massive Havel and Alexander  checked both were mentioned by previous  speakers  it was a cleaning lady it was a cleaning  lady who was prohibited any public  appearance or performance and suddenly  she occurred on the balcony on the Venza  square and now she is singing the most  famous song of 1968 the prayer for  Martin it was actually the Czech  humanist and philosopher who gave her  the lyrics Comenius the teacher of the  nations let me quote these are the words  of the song which became one of the  symbols of the revolution  besides the angel the second one let  peace remain with this country  Meli's and we spite fear and strife let  those parts let them quickly finally  pass away now that you're lost  governance of your affairs is returning  to you people is returning the cloud is  slowly flowing away from the sky and  everybody is harvesting what Dave song  but my prayer speak to the hearts which  by the time of hire  we're not burnt like flowers by frost  like by frost those words were used by  Vassar Havel in his first presidential  speech on 1st of January 1990 and I know  that Vassar Havel is an inspiration to  all of us and I understand why in the  speech he gave to the European  Parliament in Strasbourg in March 1994  he said this referring to the European  Union if the citizens of Europe  understand that this is not just an  anonymous bureaucratic monster to limit  or even deny their autonomy but simply a  new type of human community that  actually broadens their freedoms  significantly then the European Union  need not to fear for its future the task  is convincing our citizens that it is  indeed what Europe is about it is still  relevant as ever and I think that we all  could get it here today need to embody  the essence of this endeavor as human  rather than anonymous or bureaucratic  today we are far from being perfect we  face problems but of different nature  than before most importantly we do not  face them alone but together in our  European family it is a testimony to the  European nature of the anniversary that  we are celebrating it in a prominent  company of the two European  commissioners in the seats of the  European Commission with a crowd of  Czechs Slovaks and internationals we  learned many lessons in 1989 we need to  remember those lessons not in nostalgia  not 